WEBVTT

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:03.840
A difficult-looking question can become much clearer as

00:00:03.840 --> 00:00:05.510
soon as you draw the right picture.

00:00:05.960 --> 00:00:07.010
A line shows order.

00:00:07.400 --> 00:00:09.810
A table sets comparisons side by side.

00:00:10.120 --> 00:00:12.370
A sketch makes shapes and boundaries visible.

00:00:12.840 --> 00:00:14.850
An equation holds a relationship in place.

00:00:15.480 --> 00:00:17.020
Choose the one that helps you see what the

00:00:17.020 --> 00:00:18.030
question is asking.

00:00:19.010 --> 00:00:19.860
Picture a fence.

00:00:20.430 --> 00:00:23.150
Your eye can follow every post or every panel

00:00:23.150 --> 00:00:24.280
between two posts.

00:00:24.950 --> 00:00:27.580
A sketch gives both counts a place on the same line.

00:00:27.970 --> 00:00:29.870
The words each end tell us where the fence

00:00:29.870 --> 00:00:31.140
starts and stops.

00:00:31.790 --> 00:00:32.900
Mark those ends first.

00:00:33.490 --> 00:00:35.360
Then look for what repeats between them.

00:00:36.140 --> 00:00:38.410
A straight fence is thirty metres long.

00:00:38.800 --> 00:00:41.120
There is a post at each end, and the posts are

00:00:41.120 --> 00:00:42.170
five metres apart.

00:00:42.740 --> 00:00:43.830
How many posts are there?

00:00:44.340 --> 00:00:45.550
Draw the full thirty metres.

00:00:46.120 --> 00:00:48.560
Mark the left end as zero, and mark off spaces

00:00:48.560 --> 00:00:49.670
of five metres.

00:00:52.460 --> 00:00:55.040
Thirty metres divided into five-metre spaces

00:00:55.040 --> 00:00:56.250
gives six gaps.

00:00:56.900 --> 00:00:58.870
Follow each gap to its right-hand end.

00:00:59.360 --> 00:01:00.650
That accounts for six posts.

00:01:01.300 --> 00:01:03.690
The post at zero sits before the first gap,

00:01:04.160 --> 00:01:04.830
so add it once.

00:01:05.500 --> 00:01:09.050
Six gaps, plus that starting post, makes seven posts.

00:01:10.230 --> 00:01:13.490
Now join the ends to make a closed fence, using the same

00:01:13.490 --> 00:01:14.620
six equal panels.

00:01:15.310 --> 00:01:18.000
Watch the last post come back to the very first one.

00:01:18.490 --> 00:01:19.860
They meet at a single corner.

00:01:20.290 --> 00:01:22.290
Each panel now ends at a different post,

00:01:22.290 --> 00:01:24.260
and all posts are included.

00:01:24.830 --> 00:01:26.840
That makes six gaps and six posts.

00:01:28.000 --> 00:01:30.760
Try the same kind of line with a queue.

00:01:30.760 --> 00:01:33.330
Sam is ninth from the front and sixth from the back.

00:01:33.860 --> 00:01:34.810
How many people are there?

00:01:35.260 --> 00:01:37.970
Eight stand in front of Sam, and five stand behind.

00:01:38.620 --> 00:01:41.490
Eight, plus Sam, plus five, makes fourteen.

00:01:42.140 --> 00:01:45.140
Both ranks include Sam, so give Sam one place

00:01:45.140 --> 00:01:45.930
in the picture.

00:01:47.240 --> 00:01:49.610
A table helps when there are several things to compare.

00:01:50.320 --> 00:01:53.130
Box A contains eight pencils and costs sixteen dollars.

00:01:53.780 --> 00:01:56.910
Box B contains four pencils and costs twelve dollars.

00:01:57.540 --> 00:01:59.170
Add a price per pencil column.

00:01:59.640 --> 00:02:01.530
The divisions give two dollars and three dollars.

00:02:01.940 --> 00:02:04.950
Box A costs more for the box and less for each pencil.

00:02:05.810 --> 00:02:07.360
A sketch reveals a boundary.

00:02:08.190 --> 00:02:11.180
Join two mats, each four metres by three metres,

00:02:11.310 --> 00:02:12.960
along their three-metre sides.

00:02:13.670 --> 00:02:15.100
How much edge is exposed?

00:02:15.870 --> 00:02:18.080
Each mat has fourteen metres around it.

00:02:18.490 --> 00:02:20.460
The pair has twenty-eight before joining.

00:02:21.050 --> 00:02:23.740
The shared edge removes three metres from each mat,

00:02:23.930 --> 00:02:26.760
so the equation gives twenty-two metres outside.

00:02:27.900 --> 00:02:30.560
Choose a line for order and gaps, a table for

00:02:30.560 --> 00:02:33.190
comparisons, and a sketch for shape and boundaries.

00:02:34.100 --> 00:02:36.650
Use an equation to show how the quantities fit together.

00:02:37.360 --> 00:02:37.910
Your turn.

00:02:38.140 --> 00:02:41.100
Trees are planted every four metres along a forty-metre

00:02:41.100 --> 00:02:43.030
path, including both ends.

00:02:43.760 --> 00:02:44.910
How many trees are there?

00:02:45.300 --> 00:02:45.930
Sketch it first.

